benchmark_selection_with_low_rank_imputation/program_1\", \"program_id\": \"program_1\", \"type\": \"metric\", \"value\": 0.21986663130298853}\nresult.total_attempts {\"id\": \"result.total_attempts\", \"type\": \"count\", \"value\": 3}\nresult.total_correct_programs {\"id\": \"result.total_correct_programs\", \"type\": \"count\", \"value\": 3}\nresult.total_evaluated_programs {\"id\": \"result.total_evaluated_programs\", \"type\": \"count\", \"value\": 3}\nresult.total_ideas {\"id\": \"result.total_ideas\", \"type\": \"count\", \"value\": 3}\n[\n{\n\"attempt_dir_name\": \"idea_root\",\n\"attempt_id\": \"c02_robust_stacked_wrapper_selection_attempt_01\",\n\"combined_score\": 0.24671419726711147,\n\"correct\": true,\n\"eligible\": true,\n\"eval_index\": 2,\n\"evaluation_evidence\": {\n\"candidate_diagnostics_trust\": \"unverified\",\n\"episode_count\": 2,\n\"episodes\": [\n{\n\"correct\": true,\n\"duration_seconds\": 140.88599891100603,\n\"score\": 0.2428556159412707,\n\"seed\": 11\n},\n{\n\"correct\": true,\n\"duration_seconds\": 142.17663234798238,\n\"score\": 0.25057277859295224,\n\"seed\": 12\n}\n],\n\"path\": \"work/results/r03/claude/20261111_111139/investigation/ideas/02_wrapper_cv_error_benchmark_selection_with_robust_quantile_transformed_stacked_imputation/program_1/evaluation_evidence.json\",\n\"sha256\": \"sha256:f15ebbc7ebc8bfccae3c13ba12b9f19d14d639c9adcdbf3807fcd68df67ad51b\",\n\"status\": \"available\"\n},\n\"idea_dir_name\": \"02_wrapper_cv_error_benchmark_selection_with_robust_quantile_transformed_stacked_imputation\",\n\"idea_id\": \"c02_robust_stacked_wrapper_selection\",\n\"program_dir\": \"work/results/r03/claude/20261111_111139/investigation/ideas/02_wrapper_cv_error_benchmark_selection_with_robust_quantile_transformed_stacked_imputation/program_1\",\n\"program_id\": \"program_1\",\n\"program_idx\": 1\n},\n{\n\"attempt_dir_name\": \"idea_root\",\n\"attempt_id\": \"c03_monotone_latent_skill_aopt_stack_attempt_01\",\n\"combined_score\": 0.24671419726711147,\n\"correct\": true,\n\"eligible\": true,\n\"eval_index\": 3,\n\"evaluation_evidence\": {\n\"candidate_diagnostics_trust\": \"unverified\",\n\"episode_count\": 2,\n\"episodes\": [\n{\n\"correct\": true,\n\"duration_seconds\": 331.1545805450005,\n\"score\": 0.2428556159412707,\n\"seed\": 11\n},\n{\n\"correct\": true,\n\"duration_seconds\": 334.25262934599596,\n\"score\": 0.25057277859295224,\n\"seed\": 12\n}\n],\n\"path\": \"work/results/r03/claude/20261111_111139/investigation/ideas/03_robust_monotone_link_latent_skill_predictor_with_a_optimal_selection_added_to_the_incumbent_stack/program_1/evaluation_evidence.json\",\n\"sha256\": \"sha256:376024dc5d9fa85dbf35416d580f54d8ebe988b992ded4a40ce3fe35730e1aef\",\n\"status\": \"available\"\n},\n\"idea_dir_name\": \"03_robust_monotone_link_latent_skill_predictor_with_a_optimal_selection_added_to_the_incumbent_stack\",\n\"idea_id\": \"c03_monotone_latent_skill_aopt_stack\",\n\"program_dir\": \"work/results/r03/claude/20261111_111139/investigation/ideas/03_robust_monotone_link_latent_skill_predictor_with_a_optimal_selection_added_to_the_incumbent_stack/program_1\",\n\"program_id\": \"program_1\",\n\"program_idx\": 1\n},\n{\n\"attempt_dir_name\": \"idea_root\",\n\"attempt_id\": \"c01_greedy_condvar_lowrank_selection_attempt_01\",\n\"combined_score\": 0.21986663130298853,\n\"correct\": true,\n\"eligible\": true,\n\"eval_index\": 1,\n\"evaluation_evidence\": {\n\"candidate_diagnostics_trust\": \"unverified\",\n\"episode_count\": 2,\n\"episodes\": [\n{\n\"correct\": true,\n\"duration_seconds\": 95.42571800301084,\n\"score\": 0.220065704766152,\n\"seed\": 11\n},\n{\n\"correct\": true,\n\"duration_seconds\": 97.32865135399334,\n\"score\": 0.21966755783982506,\n\"seed\": 12\n}\n],\n\"path\": \"work/resu\n{'attempts': 3, 'correct_programs': 3, 'evaluated_programs': 3, 'ideas': 3} {'ablations': {'path': 'work/results/r03/claude/20261111_111139/ablations', 'status': 'missing'}, 'investigation': {'path': 'work/results/r03/claude/20261111_111139/investigation', 'status': 'included'}}\nThe proposed pair scores 0.247 against 0.220 for the simple pair, with non-overlapping per-seed ranges (0.243--0.251 vs.\\ 0.220), and also shows a larger skill over random-$k$ selection (0.132 vs.\\ 0.100). % @evidence[[analysis/search_metrics.json#02]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#result.top_program_01]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#result.top_program_03]]\nBecause several components change together and the two seeds share one held-out set, this licenses only the statement that the richer configuration was better on that partition; it does not attribute the gain to the transform, the wrapper selector or the stack. The pair extended with a latent-skill member and a latent A-optimal selector produced the same four metrics as the proposed pair to all reported digits. The extension is kept only if it improves cross-validation error, which is consistent with it being inactive, but no diagnostic recording the choice was retained, so the cause is unknown, and we treat this configuration as a null result. The sealed evaluation was run on one of these two configurations; the exact tie means the retained scores cannot say which (Supplementary Information).\n\n\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\\label{sec:limits}\n\\paragraph{What is and is not shown.} The sealed result supports a bounded statement: on family-disjoint held-out models from three pinned leaderboards, a history-fitted selector--imputer pair predicts unrun benchmark scores with a mean relative error reduction (skill) of 0.434 against column means, and the improvement is not an artefact of the imputer's marginals since permuting the observations erases it. The evidence does not show that cross-benchmark structure is \\emph{exploited by selection}; it shows that observed scores help, and that a random subset with a ridge imputer comes within a skill of 0.016 of the pair.\n\n\\paragraph{Joint scoring and missing ablations.} Selector and imputer are scored together. No controlled experiment varied one while holding the other fixed, so the contributions of the rank-Gauss transform, the wrapper selector, the stack and the clipping are unattributed. The development-partition comparison changes many components at once. A fair selector test would pair each selector with each imputer; we did not run it.\n\n\\paragraph{Uncertainty.} Reported values are seed means. The seeds vary only the method's internal randomness (fold assignments), not the held-out models, baselines or controls, so the 0.006 spread across seeds understates sampling uncertainty over models. Held-out sets are small for MTEB (37 models) and HELM Lite (25), and the held-out groups are few (25 and 7). The 0.016 margin over random-$k$ should be viewed as indistinguishable from zero until a resampling-based interval over held-out models is available; we did not compute one, and we make no significance claim.\n\n\\paragraph{Scope.} Three leaderboards, of which two contain generative language models in small numbers of benchmarks (six and ten) and one contains text-embedding models; scales differ (accuracies, win rates, F1). Family-disjoint splitting was defined by name and content heuristics. Selection is non-adaptive; adaptive stopping or sequential selection could change the picture. Missingness in leaderboards is not random (strong models are run on more benchmarks), which violates the usual missing-at-random assumption of EM-based imputation \\citep{Rubin1976Inference}. The run-time guards in Section~\\ref{sec:impl} make behaviour hardware-dependent.\n\n\\paragraph{Unperformed analyses.} We did not run a theory check of the trace objective, a component ablation, per-family error breakdowns, comparisons with low-rank matrix completion or latent-factor imputers as full alternatives, or other leaderboards. These are not claimed.\n\n\\paragraph{Threats to validity.} (1) \\emph{Leakage through naming.} Family-disjointness relies on name normalisation, a family key and a content hash; a model re-released under an unrecognised name could cross the split. (2) \\emph{Metric scale.} Errors are in history standard deviations of each benchmark, which makes a benchmark with a narrow history spread (e.g.\\ one saturated by strong models) look harder to predict than one with a wide spread. (3) \\emph{Heterogeneous coverage.} Leaderboard missingness is related to model strength and recency; selected benchmarks that are well covered in the history may be missing for new models. (4) \\emph{Configuration identity and freezing.} The configuration was chosen on the development partition before the sealed evaluation, but no artifact records which of the two tied configurations the sealed run used or that it was run only once; the sealed numbers are not selected on, but the development partition is from the same MTEB snapshot, so the MTEB result is closer to the development distribution than the other two leaderboards, which were not used to choose among configurations. (5) \\emph{Implementation coupling.} The result is for one concrete implementation, including its tuning grids and wall-clock guards; a different implementation of the same design could behave differently.\n\n\\paragraph{Practical reading.} For an evaluator with a comparable history, a handful of well-covered benchmarks and a reasonable imputer already remove a large part of the column-mean error, further benchmarks buy progressively less, and an unavoidable residual of about 0.64 history standard deviations remains on MTEB at $k{=}20$.\n\n\\section{Conclusion}\nWe posed the cold-start question of how well $k$ benchmark runs predict the remaining scores of a model from an unseen family, and evaluated a rank-Gauss, EM-Gaussian, wrapper-selected, NNLS-stacked selector--imputer family (one member of it, not identifiable from the retained evidence) on three leaderboards with family-disjoint held-out models. The sealed evaluation completed and qualified. Pooled nRMSE is 0.562 and skill against column means is 0.434, with a clear gap to permuted controls; on MTEB the error decreases from 0.787 to 0.638 as $k$ rises from 2 to 20 with sharply diminishing returns, and error levels differ across leaderboards. The margin over random-$k$ selection with a ridge imputer is only 0.016 and cannot be assigned to selection or imputation, and the retained evidence suggests that a leave-one-out greedy selector can fail to beat random subsets. The main unresolved questions are the isolated value of selection, uncertainty over held-out models, and behaviour beyond these three leaderboards; each needs additional controlled experiments that this study did not perform.\n\n\\clearpage\n\\bibliography{references}\n\\bibliographystyle{paper}\n\nanalysis:\nprotocol_facts.json\nsearch_metrics.json\n\nsource_evidence:\nconfig_latent_ext\nconfig_proposed\nconfig_simple\nevalevidence_cfg01.json\nevalevidence_cfg02.json\nevalevidence_cfg03.json\nevaluator\nmanifests\nmetrics_cfg01.json\nmetrics_cfg02.json\nmetrics_cfg03.json\n\nsource_evidence/config_latent_ext:\nexperiments.py\nprogram.py\n\nsource_evidence/config_proposed:\nexperiments.py\nprogram.py\n\nsource_evidence/config_simple:\nexperiments.py\nprogram.py\n\nsource_evidence/evaluator:\nevaluate.py\nevaluate_final.py\n\nsource_evidence/manifests:\nhelm_final_manifest.json\nmteb_final_manifest.json\nmteb_search_manifest.json\noll_final_manifest.json","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
